# twelve lines above the offender. One of the three was not cosmetic:
# `TABLE_WIDTH + 600 <= OPEN_WIDTH` constrained the window with the old
# TreeView's column sum, so narrowing OPEN_WIDTH to a value the real grid floor
# permits would have gone red about a widget that no longer exists. The
# assertion now names GRID_MIN_WIDTH, which is what it means.
#
# COLUMNS ITSELF SURVIVES AS A DECLARATION, NOT AS GEOMETRY: its `field` entries
# are still the fields a card must render, which is what the store contract
# checks. Its width and expand entries are read by nothing and are kept only as
# the record of what the TreeView drew.

# HOW THE GRID IS ORDERED, and the chips that choose it. The TreeView sorted
# by clicking a column header; a grid of cards has no headers to click, so the
# ordering has to be an explicit control or it is simply lost.
#
# FOUR KEYS AND BOTH DIRECTIONS, NOT THE MOCKUP'S THREE. Audited by
# <agent> 2026-08-21: the mockup draws three chips, the TreeView
# reached SIX orders, and "three chips replace column-click sorting" read as
# parity because 3 equals 3. `set_headers_visible(True)` plus
# `column.set_sort_column_id(index)` in the column loop gave three distinct
# keys (Package, Size, State, since two COLUMNS entries mapped to field 0) and
# a header re-click toggled each one, so 3 x 2 = 6. The chips had 3 with no
# direction state at all. Restored here: `state` is the key that had no chip,
# and every chip now flips on a second press, which is exactly what re-clicking
# a header did. Net 4 x 2 = 8.
#
# ONE OLD ORDER IS DELIBERATELY NOT RESTORED. The TreeView's Size column sorted
# field 6, which holds `repo.human_size(...)`, so it was LEXICOGRAPHIC OVER A
# HUMAN STRING and put "9.9 kB" after "10.1 MB". The chip keys on the numeric
# `candidate["size"]`, so it is the first correct size order this window has
# had. Restoring the old one would restore a defect.
#
